## How to answer (this is the two-way interface)

Reply **inline, directly under the question**, on a new line starting
with `> ` (a Markdown blockquote). You don't delete anything yourself.
Example:

```
- **2026-07-18 (nightly): Stalemate — reset the floor or die?**
  > reset to the start of the current floor, keep the run alive
```

Contract the commands follow:
- **`/nightly-batch` owns answer-processing.** On its next run it reads
  this file first, treats any `> ` answer as authoritative human
  direction (same standing as `FOCUS.md`) **subject to rules A-D below**,
  acts on it, folds a standing decision into `FOCUS.md`,
  then removes that question+answer block once acted on (git history and
  the run's report keep the record).
- **`/bug-sweep` only appends** genuine judgment calls; it must NOT act on
  or delete a `> ` answer (that's the nightly's job) — this keeps the fast
  15-minute loop from racing the nightly over the same file.
- Unanswered questions are left untouched and never re-asked/duplicated.
- To dismiss a question without any action, just delete its line by hand.

## How an answer binds (rules A-D, 2026-07-29)

An answer is the most authoritative input this system takes, and until
2026-07-29 it was also the only input with NO admission control: ideas
are parked by default, machine-state claims are re-probed not quoted, but
a `> ` reply was simply obeyed. That gap has a name and three exhibits --
a question can be asked from a stale premise, so a confident, correct
reply can direct action at a world that no longer exists (a project
described as parked that was never parked; a branch described as
unmerged that merged four days earlier). "The human decides" is exactly
the doctrine that makes a false-premise instruction unstoppable, because
no point in the loop is authorized to say "the thing you are deciding
about is not true." These four rules authorize it, without demoting the
answer.

**A. Answers are DIRECTION, not INSTRUCTION.** A `> ` reply states
standing intent. Whoever acts re-derives the concrete action from
CURRENT state, never from the state the question described. The question
is context for the reply, not a specification of the act. This is what
makes answer staleness a non-problem by construction rather than by
expiry timer -- there is no "too old to act on" here, and there does not
need to be.

**B. Re-probe the premise before acting.** An instruction carries claims
about system state; re-probe them like any other claim. If the premise
holds, act. If it is FALSE, split by reversibility:
- **Reversible** corrected action (a commit, a branch, a file edit) --
  act on the *intent* against real state, and flag the correction plainly
  in the report.
- **Irreversible** (a message to a person, real money, an unbacked
  delete) -- stop and surface. Same stop-and-wait bar `FOCUS.md` already
  sets; this rule adds no second one.

**C. Extract the standing direction, silently.** If a reply generalizes
past its question ("always merge", "never ask me about X"), fold the
generalization into `FOCUS.md` as a standing decision -- quoted and
dated -- WITHOUT asking first. The generalization IS the payload (rule
A), and a `FOCUS.md` entry is `git revert`-able, so it passes rule B's
reversibility switch. A standing policy left in a one-off answer slot is
either lost or silently obeyed forever unratified.

**D. No clean-check reports.** A re-probe that confirms the human was
right produces NO OUTPUT. Not a line, not a footnote. Attention is the
scarcest resource in this system and a fallible monitor that reports its
clean checks costs more attention than having no monitor at all. "I
checked and you were right" is a cost with zero information.

**The channel rule is ONE-DIRECTIONAL.** Agents must unmix -- separate
operations from obligation from identity when speaking TO the user. The
user may mix freely when speaking to agents; four streams in one breath
is the system working, not a fault to be corrected. Never read the
unmixing obligation as license to ask the user to pre-sort their input.

Questions are ADDED WITH A COMMAND, not hand-typed:

    scheduler ask <project> "<the question, in full>"

RETIRES the hand-append format this file taught until 2026-07-28
(`- **YYYY-MM-DD (nightly|bug-sweep): <question>**`). That format put the
date and the source ahead of the question, and every summary view shows
an entry's bold span -- so ten questions rendered as ten near-identical
stubs of date + provenance, and scheduler's own file reached 15 entries
that could not be read at a glance. `scheduler ask` stamps the id, date
and provenance itself and puts the question first structurally, so the
shape cannot be gotten wrong. `bin/questions-lint.sh` FLAGs hand-written
entries in `scheduler sweep`.

Add a `  > (answer inline here)` placeholder line under the generated
entry so the reply slot stays obvious, plus short context if needed.
